# Units that have no work in a container and fail there. Each gets a drop-in,
# /usr/lib/systemd/system/<unit>.d/omanywhere-container.conf, with
# ConditionVirtualization=!container: skipped in a container, unchanged
# anywhere else. The reason goes into the drop-in as its comment. A unit named
# user/<unit> is a user unit, and its drop-in goes under /usr/lib/systemd/user.
#
# Several also fail for a reason that is systemd's: a unit with User= and a
# system call filter fails at step USER without CAP_SYS_ADMIN ("Failed to keep
# CAP_SYS_ADMIN", systemd issue #34565). That's no reason to skip a unit the
# desktop needs (polkit.conf fixes polkit instead); each one here is skipped
# because it has no job in a container.
#
# unit                          reason
bluetooth.service               no Bluetooth controller in a container; the host owns the radios
docker.service                  Docker inside an unprivileged container can't work (no cgroup delegation, mounts or iptables)
docker.socket                   would start docker.service (above) on first use
limine-snapper-sync.service     no Limine boot menu or btrfs snapshots in a container; the host boots the machine
linux-modules-cleanup.service   no kernel in the image; the container runs on the host's kernel and modules
power-profiles-daemon.service   the host manages power; a container can't set CPU or platform power profiles
sddm.service                    omanywhere-session.service starts the desktop instead; sddm waits for a graphical seat, which logind can't see in a container (no udev), and on one its greeter would take the host's displays
snapper-cleanup.service         no btrfs snapshots in a container (the root is overlayfs), so nothing to clean up
snapper-cleanup.timer           starts snapper-cleanup.service (above)
systemd-oomd.service            the host's OOM handling covers the container, whose memory limit Docker sets
systemd-oomd.socket             would start systemd-oomd.service (above)
systemd-resolved.service        Docker gives the container its DNS in /etc/resolv.conf
systemd-resolved-monitor.socket would start systemd-resolved.service (above)
systemd-resolved-varlink.socket would start systemd-resolved.service (above)
upower.service                  the host manages power; a container has no batteries or power supplies of its own (it would report the host's), and its PrivateUsers= sandbox needs a user namespace, which Docker's seccomp profile denies (polkit.conf's capset filter doesn't help; only turning that sandbox off would)
user/xdg-document-portal.service Flatpak's document portal mounts a FUSE file system at /run/user/UID/doc; an unprivileged container has no /dev/fuse and can't mount (no CAP_SYS_ADMIN), so it can never work here
ufw.service                     the host's firewall governs the container's network; netfilter needs NET_ADMIN, which the container lacks
